IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cc-accused`

-- Criminal Code -- worked cases, from the seat of the accused person.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (C2026C00243), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the
-- facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Findings of the tribunal of fact are given as facts.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 2.2. HYPOTHETICAL: Ria is charged under s 474.17 for messages sent in 2025: a Code offence.
#ASSERT `Does Chapter 2 govern my offence question` TRUE (Date 1 3 2025) EQUALS `answered` `yes: Chapter 2 applies to every offence against the Code (s 2.2(1))`
-- A Taxation Administration Act offence committed on 15 December 2001: Chapter 2 applies; the day before: not covered.
#ASSERT `Does Chapter 2 govern my offence question` FALSE (Date 15 12 2001) EQUALS `answered` `yes: Chapter 2 applies to this Commonwealth offence outside the Code, because my conduct was on or after 15 December 2001 (s 2.2(2))`
#ASSERT `Does Chapter 2 govern my offence question` FALSE (Date 14 12 2001) EQUALS `not covered` `my offence is not in the Code and my conduct was before 15 December 2001: Chapter 2 does not apply to it (s 2.2(2)), and the common law principles that then applied are not encoded`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 5.6 defaults. s 474.17(1)(a), using a carriage service (conduct, no fault stated): intention.
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `conduct` `none stated` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `intention: the offence states no fault element for this conduct element, so intention is the fault element (s 5.6(1))`
-- s 474.17(1)(b), read as a circumstance: recklessness.
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `a circumstance` `none stated` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `recklessness: the offence states no fault element for this circumstance or result, so recklessness is the fault element, and proof of intention or knowledge also satisfies it (ss 5.6(2), 5.4(4))`
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `a result of conduct` `none stated` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `recklessness: the offence states no fault element for this circumstance or result, so recklessness is the fault element, and proof of intention or knowledge also satisfies it (ss 5.6(2), 5.4(4))`
-- s 135.1(5)(c), the Commonwealth entity element: absolute liability (s 135.1(6)).
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `a circumstance` `none stated` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `none: absolute liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, and a mistake of fact under s 9.2 is no defence (s 6.2)`
-- A strict liability element.
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `a circumstance` `none stated` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `none: strict liability applies to this element, so no fault need be proved, but my reasonable mistake of fact under s 9.2 is a defence (s 6.1)`
-- s 135.1(5)(b) states knowledge.
#ASSERT `What fault must be proved for this element question` `a result of conduct` `knowledge` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the fault element the offence states for it (ss 3.1(3), 5.1)`

-- ss 5.2-5.5. Recklessness as to a circumstance: aware of substantial risk, unjustifiable: met. Aware but justifiable: not.
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a circumstance` `recklessness` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a circumstance` `recklessness` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- Recklessness is satisfied by intention (belief that the circumstance existed) (s 5.4(4)).
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a circumstance` `recklessness` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Intention as to conduct: meant it.
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `conduct` `intention` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `conduct` `intention` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Intention as to a result: aware it will occur in the ordinary course (s 5.2(3)).
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a result of conduct` `intention` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Knowledge requires awareness in the ordinary course; awareness of a risk is not enough.
#ASSERT NOT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a circumstance` `knowledge` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- Negligence turns on the tribunal's finding alone; intention does not satisfy it in the text (finding 4).
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a result of conduct` `negligence` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a result of conduct` `negligence` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- Knowledge is not defined for conduct (finding 3): not met even with every awareness. Recklessness as to conduct is met by
-- intention (s 5.4(4)).
#ASSERT NOT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `conduct` `knowledge` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `conduct` `recklessness` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- No fault element (strict or absolute): nothing to prove.
#ASSERT `Did I have the fault element for this element` `a circumstance` `none stated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- s 4.2. HYPOTHETICAL: Ben sent a message while sleep-walking (not willed): not voluntary. Drunk: s 4.2(6).
#ASSERT `Was my conduct voluntary question` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: my conduct was not a product of my will (for example a spasm, or an act in sleep or unconsciousness), or I could not have done the act omitted or controlled the state of affairs, so it cannot be a physical element of the offence (s 4.2)`
#ASSERT `Was my conduct voluntary question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was self-induced intoxication, which cannot be considered on voluntariness (s 4.2(6))`
#ASSERT `Was my conduct voluntary question` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, for this purpose: what made it unwilled was a mental impairment, which cannot be used to deny voluntariness; consider s 7.3 instead (s 7.3(6))`
#ASSERT `Was my conduct voluntary question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: my conduct was a product of my will`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 7.1, 7.2. Ages 9, 10, 13 and 14.
#ASSERT `Can I be responsible at my age question` 9 TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I was under 10, and a child under 10 is not criminally responsible for an offence (s 7.1)`
#ASSERT `Can I be responsible at my age question` 10 FALSE TRUE EQUALS `only if the prosecution proves I knew my conduct was wrong: I was 10 or more but under 14 (s 7.2)`
#ASSERT `Can I be responsible at my age question` 13 FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I was 10 or more but under 14, and it is not proved that I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
#ASSERT `Can I be responsible at my age question` 13 TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I was 10 or more but under 14, and the prosecution has proved I knew my conduct was wrong (s 7.2)`
#ASSERT `Can I be responsible at my age question` 14 FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: Division 7 sets no age bar, because I was 14 or older`
-- s 7.3. Proved impairment that stopped Ben knowing it was wrong: special verdict. Impairment with none of the effects: no.
#ASSERT `Does my mental impairment excuse me question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I am not criminally responsible; the verdict must be the special verdict, not guilty because of mental impairment (s 7.3(1), (5))`
#ASSERT `Does my mental impairment excuse me question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: it is not proved on the balance of probabilities that I had a mental impairment with one of the s 7.3(1) effects, and I am presumed not to have (s 7.3(3))`
#ASSERT `Does my mental impairment excuse me question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: it is not proved on the balance of probabilities that I had a mental impairment with one of the s 7.3(1) effects, and I am presumed not to have (s 7.3(3))`

-- Division 8. HYPOTHETICAL: Ria's drink was spiked and her messages were the result: not responsible (s 8.5).
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` TRUE TRUE `whether I intended my conduct (basic intent)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `I am not criminally responsible at all: my conduct was the result of intoxication that was not self-induced (s 8.5)`
-- Ria chose to drink. On whether she meant to send them (basic intent): cannot be considered.
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE TRUE `whether I intended my conduct (basic intent)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on this question (ss 4.2(6), 8.2(1))`
-- On recklessness about how her messages would be regarded (not basic intent): may be considered.
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE TRUE `whether I had another fault element: intention as to a circumstance or result, knowledge or recklessness` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
-- On a reasonable-belief defence (duress): a sober reasonable person.
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE TRUE `whether my belief was reasonable, for a defence that needs a reasonable belief` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no, it does not help: the standard is that of a reasonable person who is not intoxicated (ss 8.3(1), 8.4(2))`
-- On an actual-belief defence where every element is basic intent: not considered (s 8.4(4)); otherwise considered.
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE TRUE `whether I had a belief a defence relies on` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: every physical element of this offence has a fault element of basic intent, so self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on the belief my defence relies on (s 8.4(4))`
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE TRUE `whether I had a belief a defence relies on` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, it may be considered (ss 8.2(3), (4), 8.4(1))`
-- Not self-induced but not the cause of the conduct: on negligence, a reasonable person intoxicated to the same extent.
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` TRUE FALSE `whether I was negligent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the standard is that of a reasonable person intoxicated to the same extent as I was, because my intoxication was not self-induced (ss 8.3(2), 8.4(3))`
#ASSERT `Can my intoxication help me question` FALSE FALSE `whether my conduct was voluntary` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: evidence of self-induced intoxication cannot be considered on this question (ss 4.2(6), 8.2(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Division 9. HYPOTHETICAL: Dev kept a Centrelink payment believing he was entitled. Under a fault element, an honest
-- mistake that negates dishonesty excuses, reasonable or not (s 9.1).
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `by a fault element other than negligence` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: my mistaken belief or ignorance of the facts negates the fault element, so I am not criminally responsible; it need not have been reasonable (s 9.1)`
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `by a fault element other than negligence` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: my mistake or ignorance does not negate the fault element (s 9.1)`
-- Strict liability: needs consideration, reasonableness and no offence on the believed facts (s 9.2).
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `with strict liability` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I considered the facts and my mistaken belief was reasonable, and on the facts as I believed them there was no offence (s 9.2)`
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `with strict liability` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: for a strict liability element a mistake excuses only if I considered whether the facts existed, my belief was reasonable, and on those facts there would have been no offence (s 9.2)`
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `with strict liability` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: for a strict liability element a mistake excuses only if I considered whether the facts existed, my belief was reasonable, and on those facts there would have been no offence (s 9.2)`
-- Absolute liability: never (Dev thought the payer was a private company: s 135.1(5)(c)).
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `with absolute liability` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: mistake of fact is no defence to an absolute liability element (s 6.2)`
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of fact excuse me question` `by negligence` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: Division 9 gives no mistake of fact defence for an element of negligence; whether I was negligent is decided by s 5.5`
-- ss 9.3, 9.4. Ignorance of the Act: no excuse. Of an unpublished instrument: excuse. Of a published instrument: no.
#ASSERT NOT `Does my mistake of law excuse me` `an Act` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of law excuse me` `subordinate legislation (a regulation, rule or other legislative instrument)` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does my mistake of law excuse me` `subordinate legislation (a regulation, rule or other legislative instrument)` FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Does my mistake of law excuse me` `an Act` TRUE FALSE
-- s 9.5. Dev took back a laptop he mistakenly believed was his: excused. Had he punched someone to get it: no.
#ASSERT `Does my claim of right excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Does my claim of right excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does my claim of right excuse me` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Division 10. s 10.1: a strict liability element brought about by a hacker on Ria's account, not guardable against.
#ASSERT `Does intervening conduct or an event excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does intervening conduct or an event excuse me` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does intervening conduct or an event excuse me` TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- s 10.2: Ria threatened into sending messages; all three reasonable beliefs: excused. She had joined the threatener's group
-- to do such things: not.
#ASSERT `Does duress excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Does duress excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does duress excuse me` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- s 10.3.
#ASSERT `Does sudden or extraordinary emergency excuse me` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does sudden or extraordinary emergency excuse me` TRUE FALSE TRUE
-- s 10.4. Ben strikes an attacker, believing it necessary, a reasonable response as he perceived it: excused.
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `defend myself or another person` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I believed my conduct was necessary for a purpose s 10.4(2) allows, and it was a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them, so I am not criminally responsible (s 10.4(1), (2))`
-- Defending a person, even with intentional really serious injury: s 10.4(3) does not bar it; the reasonable-response test does.
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `defend myself or another person` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I believed my conduct was necessary for a purpose s 10.4(2) allows, and it was a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them, so I am not criminally responsible (s 10.4(1), (2))`
-- Protecting property with intentional really serious injury: barred.
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `protect property from unlawful taking, destruction, damage or interference` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury to protect property or deal with a trespass, and self-defence never covers that (s 10.4(3))`
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `remove someone committing criminal trespass from land or premises` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I intentionally inflicted death or really serious injury to protect property or deal with a trespass, and self-defence never covers that (s 10.4(3))`
-- Resisting an arrest he knew was lawful.
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `prevent or end the unlawful imprisonment of myself or another person` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: I was responding to conduct I knew was lawful (s 10.4(4))`
-- Not a reasonable response.
#ASSERT `Does self-defence excuse me question` `defend myself or another person` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I did not believe my conduct was necessary for that purpose, or it was not a reasonable response in the circumstances as I perceived them (s 10.4(2))`
-- s 10.5.
#ASSERT `Does lawful authority excuse me` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does lawful authority excuse me` FALSE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Part 2.6.
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `an element of the offence` FALSE EQUALS `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt (ss 13.1(1), 13.2(1))`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `a defence in Part 2.3 (other than mental impairment), such as duress or self-defence` FALSE EQUALS `I bear only an evidential burden: I must point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it (from my own evidence, the prosecution's or the court's); then the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt (s 13.3)`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `a defence in Part 2.3 (other than mental impairment), such as duress or self-defence` TRUE EQUALS `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt: I have pointed to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it, so the prosecution must disprove it (ss 13.1(2), 13.2, 13.3)`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `an exception, exemption, excuse, qualification or justification in the law creating the offence` FALSE EQUALS `I bear only an evidential burden: I must point to evidence suggesting a reasonable possibility of it (from my own evidence, the prosecution's or the court's); then the prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt (s 13.3)`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `a matter the law expressly requires me to prove, calls a legal burden, or presumes unless I prove otherwise` TRUE EQUALS `I bear a legal burden, on the balance of probabilities (ss 13.4, 13.5)`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `mental impairment` FALSE EQUALS `whoever relies on it, on the balance of probabilities; I am presumed not to have had one (s 7.3(3))`
#ASSERT `Who must prove it question` `that I knew my conduct was wrong, when I was 10 to 13` FALSE EQUALS `the prosecution, beyond reasonable doubt (ss 13.1(1), 13.2(1))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 474.17. Ria sent repeated threatening messages to an ex-partner; the court finds reasonable persons would regard them as
-- menacing; she was aware of the risk and taking it was unjustifiable; no defence: made out, 5 years maximum.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if proved: every element and fault element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 5 years (s 474.17(1))`
-- A rude but not offensive post (the court does not so find).
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the court does not find that reasonable persons would regard my use, in all the circumstances, as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 474.17(1)(b), 473.4)`
-- Not aware of any risk that it would be so regarded.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I was not reckless (nor knew or intended) that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 5.6(2), 5.4)`
-- Her phone was used by someone else (she did not mean to use it).
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I did not use a carriage service, or did not mean to (ss 474.17(1)(a), 5.6(1))`
-- Duress.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
-- Fork F1 (au:REQ-0103): both readings of s 474.17(1)(b), selectable. The case that separates them: Ria meant to send the
-- messages, the court finds reasonable persons would regard them as offensive, but she was not aware of any risk that they
-- would be so regarded. Reading (a), a circumstance with recklessness: not guilty. Reading (b), part of the conduct with
-- intention: made out.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I was not reckless (nor knew or intended) that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 5.6(2), 5.4)`
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if proved: every element and fault element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 5 years (s 474.17(1))`
-- Reading (b): she meant to use her phone but not to send that content that way (a message sent to the wrong group by an
-- autocomplete she did not intend): not guilty on the conduct reading. On reading (a) the same facts, with awareness of the
-- risk, are made out; the "meant to use it in the way I did" input is not read there.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I did not mean to use the carriage service in the way I did (by that method or with that content); on the conduct reading that is the only fault element for paragraph (1)(b) (ss 474.17(1)(b), 5.6(1); fork F1)`
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if proved: every element and fault element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 5 years (s 474.17(1))`
-- Both readings agree where the court does not find the use offensive, or a defence applies.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the court does not find that reasonable persons would regard my use, in all the circumstances, as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 474.17(1)(b), 473.4)`
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence on a reading question` `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
-- The original question is reading (a): on the separating case it answers "not reckless", as reading (a) does above.
#ASSERT `Have I committed the s 474.17 offence question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I was not reckless (nor knew or intended) that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive (ss 5.6(2), 5.4)`
-- What must be proved about her state of mind, on each reading.
#ASSERT `What must be proved about my state of mind under s 474.17 question` `(a) a circumstance, carrying recklessness (s 5.6(2)) -- the default reading` EQUALS `that I meant to use the carriage service, and that I was aware of a substantial risk that reasonable persons would regard my use as menacing, harassing or offensive and, knowing the circumstances, it was unjustifiable to take that risk (or that I knew or intended it) (ss 5.6(1), (2), 5.4)`
#ASSERT `What must be proved about my state of mind under s 474.17 question` `(b) part of the conduct, carrying intention (s 5.6(1))` EQUALS `only that I meant to use the carriage service in the way I did (meant to send what I sent, by that method); whether reasonable persons would regard it as menacing, harassing or offensive is for the court or jury, with no awareness of it required of me (ss 5.6(1), 4.1)`

-- s 135.1. Dev invented a dependant to get a higher Centrelink payment (a gain from Services Australia), dishonest by ordinary
-- standards and he knew it: made out, 10 years maximum.
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `obtaining a gain (s 135.1(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if proved: every element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 10 years (s 135.1)`
-- He honestly thought he was entitled (not dishonest to his knowledge).
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `obtaining a gain (s 135.1(1))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: what I did was not dishonest by the standards of ordinary people, or I did not know it was (s 130.3)`
-- The payer was a private insurer, not a Commonwealth entity.
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `obtaining a gain (s 135.1(1))` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the other person is not a Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(b), (3)(b), (5)(c))`
-- Causing a loss without intending one.
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `causing a loss (s 135.1(3))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I did not act with the intention of obtaining a gain from, or causing a loss to, the Commonwealth entity (s 135.1(1)(a), (3)(a))`
-- s 135.1(5): caused a risk of loss knowing of a substantial risk: made out; without that knowledge: not.
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `causing a loss or risk of loss I knew or believed would happen (s 135.1(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if proved: every element is made out and no defence applies; the maximum penalty is imprisonment for 10 years (s 135.1)`
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `causing a loss or risk of loss I knew or believed would happen (s 135.1(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I did not cause a loss or risk of loss, or did not know or believe that it would occur or that there was a substantial risk of it (s 135.1(5)(a), (b))`
#ASSERT `Have I committed general dishonesty question` `obtaining a gain (s 135.1(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a defence in Part 2.3 applies, so I am not criminally responsible`
